p.src:
To compare with Schwarzschild, choose units in which $2MG=1$. The radial part of Eq. [eq:l6-schwarzschild] is $d\tau^{2}
=
\frac{r-1}{r}\,dt^{2}
-
\frac{r}{r-1}\,dr^{2}.$ Very near $r=1$, the small numerator $r-1$ must be retained, while the otherwise harmless factors of $r$ may be replaced by one. Thus $d\tau^{2}
\simeq
(r-1)\,dt^{2}
-
\frac{dr^{2}}{r-1}.$ With $\xi=r-1,$ this is exactly Eq. [eq:l6-xi-metric]. The near-horizon Schwarzschild geometry is locally the Rindler description of flat spacetime.

